{"title":"2011 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> has side-lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/d/c8d65241c0611ddc2de1b3aef77be2547c842620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = 12, BC = 24,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"154\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9195b8f3be608ad05e52dca2de7ab73df50198b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = 18.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"73\" height=\"13\" /> The line through the incenter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> parallel to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4a6d41204181432504f1108ded79e4a49e0e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> What is the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/a/f7aa3e04934d1b5c353b7e4edf3ef4a3a73de6e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN?$\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/2/4521b95ba2a5e4e9200da81da02d94a086742ae8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 27 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 30 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\  33 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 36 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 42$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the incenter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/8/1f828f10cad9235a2c72c34369f5fd4a1179e579.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ABC}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/d/b0d8f83bc78312dfba02faa80ee7558a951b0b9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{MO} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/2/5d29ab3cedcc24c862f97e5d7f2bb1da18b7067e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BO}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> is the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/0/0d089768b09c8f192985935b4efd1d34ab6094ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ABC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />, we have \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/0/4305bb6699bacdcba757e231d193c6641d876115.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle{MBO} = \\angle{CBO} = \\angle{MOB} = \\frac{1}{2}\\angle{MBC}\\]\" width=\"326\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>It then follows due to alternate interior angles and base angles of isosceles triangles that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/e/55e4e088193854985a30e37cfca676f5a59a3540.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MO = MB$\" width=\"92\" height=\"12\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/d/c4d2e382013fb4a007bc555a1e62f7d16c80c3a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$NO = NC$\" width=\"86\" height=\"12\" />. The perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/2/39261b5a15ee2bfcd406ba74a71cbdb49f982568.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{AMN}$\" width=\"66\" height=\"13\" /> then becomes\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/8/b38a9104edaff82037e5d5b8b9d34a0cb120970e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} AM + MN + NA &amp;= AM + MO + NO + NA \\\\ &amp;= AM + MB + NC + NA \\\\ &amp;= AB + AC \\\\ &amp;= 30 \\rightarrow \\boxed{(B)} \\end{align*}\" width=\"363\" height=\"105\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the incenter. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> is the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/e/21e5827c549c40537c55853b73f1076a26ba7840.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle MAN$\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />. Let the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> meets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3ad57710b6810476e6017ea2c2cc6d41219c218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> meets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />. By applying both angle bisector theorem and Menelaus' theorem,\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db70e75e34fe0ea69193c554cb7c55d4df5f1f4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{OP} \\times \\frac{BP}{BC} \\times \\frac{CQ}{QA} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"175\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/f/c2fd48de464992c65d35b5d4d46fa31c5b60fbf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{OP} \\times \\frac{12}{30} \\times \\frac{24}{12} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"153\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/5/535d269952c7f47771da441f7d0950f52baa51b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{OP}=\\frac{5}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d7449425f5adea7bb5f5e5c59bc2b95f7532a8be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{AP}=\\frac{5}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\nPerimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/a/24ae2d2fe892420b1221427e8165c3efaf8678d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN = \\frac{12+24+18}{9} \\times 5 = 30  \\rightarrow \\boxed{(B)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"335\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the incenter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> intersect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. By the angle bisector theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/0/760fa071996a15fd01cc234b03f03555580bdb0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{AC} = \\frac{12}{18} = \\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"173\" height=\"38\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/d/40d03ae3adfa46ffb1a6a707ccc9d3b0cf67e61e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD+DC = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"124\" height=\"14\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/8/3a86b3505c21929447560d6d72a8bcf1de6f8310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BD}{24-BD} = \\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d74fa0d3c9ee7dee3b28d30973ea674ffaf4eca7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3BD = 48 - 2BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"143\" height=\"13\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/f/78f6c0b0f95cd2c87d6d3c10c91b282f461e4c5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD = \\frac{48}{5} = 9.6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"123\" height=\"37\" />. By the angle bisector theorem on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/6/db69fdc744b6899391040ed738f198d313a589ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{DO}{OA} = \\frac{BD}{BA} = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"176\" height=\"37\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/1509630259e62e90ee9046547f894bb9719585f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{DA}{OA} = 1 + \\frac{DO}{OA} = \\frac{9}{5} = 1.8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"206\" height=\"38\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/c/37c2a01525edc5a617dc6925ed09ba9af4d2e4f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{AD} = \\frac{5}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/1/5c1ef5c03ed500ff33e808ec4623df454f3c9ac5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN \\sim \\triangle ABC$\" width=\"149\" height=\"13\" />, the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a718b55cf3d3cf2aa67b60f9a86c3bc135b584d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/0/d50fc15baeb8166e3c6db2eb864f266ead803f80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{9} (12 + 18 + 24) = 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"168\" height=\"37\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/1/d712e44b2d06c4f9df372ce70a41b2c69d597ca1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 30}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"64\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>Another way to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/2/78293a2850c74680519bc2f2038034c665bba669.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{OD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"32\" height=\"38\" /> is to use mass points. Assign a mass of 24 to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, a mass of 18 to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and a mass of 12 to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> has mass 30, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/0/b503f50439c79130dcf482f5d5b1b499f98d31cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AO}{OD} = \\frac{30}{24} = \\frac{5}{4} = 1.25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"173\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the ratio of the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a718b55cf3d3cf2aa67b60f9a86c3bc135b584d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is the ratio of their heights, and finding the two heights is pretty easy. Note that the height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10c603896b1ed1fe34b3f76e856a34ff6f6f5154.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9\\sqrt{15}}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"43\" /> from Herons and then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/1/af1d5787835368eb921fc4f52cb8ad97bf3de11a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=\\frac{bh}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"62\" height=\"39\" /> and also that the height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is simply the height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> minus the inradius. We know the area and the semiperimeter so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a76d98c72220b00b71f463f852b342702acb4c1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\frac{A}{s}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"52\" height=\"39\" /> which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/6479fd1ed70a79f68e452297855e7bfc24a7a81d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\sqrt{15}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />. Now we know that the altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/2/d8218ec24a6d2ec9629654a737f9c665817f53de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5\\sqrt{15}}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"41\" /> so the ratios of the heights from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a718b55cf3d3cf2aa67b60f9a86c3bc135b584d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/c/e7cee4c1bc083c3979f7ac19ac9f8646a3d0a29e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Thus the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a718b55cf3d3cf2aa67b60f9a86c3bc135b584d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c398cd86502ea3796db2c1fe42e3ef43b0cd85c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{9} \\times 54 = 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"95\" height=\"37\" /> so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/e/f2e33ab9a00e021af49e888212645d4667903c49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"26\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p>-srisainandan6\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u23iWcqbJlE\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u23iWcqbJlE</a>\n~Shreyas S\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/5jwD5UViZO8?t=1013\">https://youtu.be/5jwD5UViZO8?t=1013</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A\">2011 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2011 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_3\" ti